From be1ed7a9143bd06e7d189b4e6f3763714b8a6229 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: baKon <mike.ski83@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 15:44:01 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] three ranks: pad the checkpoint, exactly, where the
 geometry does not divide

Radiance refuses a head count the world does not divide, by name, and
three is such a world for Qwen3.8-Flash-Next (24 q / 2 kv / GDN 16, 48).
Replication answers four ranks, not three. The answer at three is the
one tcclaviger's vLLM applies at load time: pad to 36 / 3 / 18 / 54 with
zero weights placed where a zero contributes nothing, so the padded model
computes what the original does. Radiance has no load-time pad, so this is
an offline tool and a document.

- tools/pad_tp.py: numpy-only, streams one tensor at a time, handles the
  Flash-Next fused q+gate head blocks and the GDN in_proj row orders,
  rewrites config.json. --sparse reflinks the original shards and writes
  only the padded tensors into shards that sort first, which the
  checkpoint reader's first-wins emplace then shadows: 6.6 GB of new
  bytes for a 336 GiB checkpoint.
- tools/pad_verify.py: every tensor read back -- untouched ones
  byte-identical, padded ones the source in place and zeros elsewhere.
- docs/TP3.md: what is padded and why each pad is exact, the tool, what
  the engine gained at three ranks, the serve line, measurements on three
  R9700s against the shipped two-rank container.
- README: the three-rank row and the document.
